From 870b0541bf06c02f9c134545e7ec6bb3464cfb88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 13:04:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/24] Send True Tone to the panel as macOS does, beside the
 night light

macOS's True Tone on the MacBook Pro 14" M4 is a full 3x3 colour matrix
sent to the panel once per frame over an eased 1.9 s ramp, and it adds up
with Night Shift. omarchy-mac-truetone moved hyprsunset's temperature
instead: a diagonal only, nothing at all until hyprsunset was running
(Omarchy starts it with the night light, not at login), and it had to
stay above 6000 K so the shell would not read it as night light.

The display driver now holds a panel white-point adaptation beside the
compositor's colour matrix and sends their product (panel_adaptation).
True Tone writes there from a system service, so the night light keeps
hyprsunset and Hyprland's CTM to itself and the two add up: adaptation
x night light, as on macOS. The white still goes 0.2 of the way from
the panel's white to the room's colour; the matrix for it is a von
Kries adaptation fitted to macOS's own matrices on this panel (every
coefficient within 0.003 of macOS's at its whites), and each move runs
macOS's per-frame curve (58 % of the move left at 0.5 s, 13 % at 1 s,
done at 1.93 s). "omarchy-mac-truetone off" gives the panel its own white
back through the same ramp; on resumes. A kernel without
panel_adaptation leaves True Tone off.
